_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Fresh plan review found one blocking child-coverage gap: parent AC7's complete legacy caller inventory had no explicit child-owned parametrized proof. Remediated in prerequisite 953a by adding the full event-append/batch/import/transition/delete/compact/claim compatibility matrix. 953a will be re-reviewed first; then this parent review will be rerun dependency-first.","timestamp":1786146587292409001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Pre-certification coherence remediation: clarified that `final-push-rejected` is a scenario through the single shared retries-exhausted terminal branch after five clean merges, not a second branch or redundant test. The AC now requires one test per actual control-flow branch plus the five-clean-merge terminal scenario, preserving the exactly-one-final-push invariant.","timestamp":1786147130775232001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Pre-certification AC consolidation: removed duplicate smoke-test criteria. The plan now names one re-homed in-process contention oracle and the SAME exactly two workflow smoke tests (one per workflow) carrying all wiring assertions together: correct action, entrypoint invoked, strict=True, REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always, persistent rejection nonzero, and no git-output pipe.","timestamp":1786147249532073001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Pre-certification mechanism remediation: D3 now pins unconditional delegation after optional commit. Added dirty-tree, clean-but-locally-ahead success, and clean/ahead persistent-rejection strict oracles so the reconciler's precommitted binding-store state cannot be silently stranded.","timestamp":1786147430753703001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Pre-certification compatibility rationale: documented why the shared 3→5 budget applies to existing best-effort callers too. They all contend on the same hot origin/tickets ref; extra cycles occur only after real non-fast-forward rejection, add no uncontended-path latency, use bounded idempotent no-sleep merges, preserve return/disposition, and avoid reintroducing strict-versus-best-effort retry drift.","timestamp":1786147586791690001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Pre-certification completeness remediation: D5 now explicitly covers every early silent return omitted from the parent enumeration—malformed destination, missing remote, permanent policy decline, and transient transport failure—with the stable child-owned reasons and paired best-effort controls.","timestamp":1786147901141556001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Execution closeout evidence: all three children are now closed through their completion gates. Strict CAS/final-push semantics landed in Gerrit 1529 (a8e0f67c82; LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, CI 31232668199). Locked dirty-tree commit-and-push landed in Gerrit 1536 (1532700efd; both votes, zero unresolved comments). Both workflow cutovers and compatibility documentation landed through Gerrit 1538/1540/1541, ending at origin/main 511c339a63 with both votes and green CI. Old library/MCP best-effort defaults and copied-template rollback continuity remain explicitly tested and documented.","timestamp":1786212643111004001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Post-completion decision-record correction for D1: child 030f ultimately RETAINED the live BRIDGE_ALERT emitter, reducer, and bridge-alert event channel; its earlier deletion plan was revised. This ticket’s shipped strict-push behavior remains correct and does not depend on deletion: strict mode adds no new BRIDGE_ALERT artifact and fails loudly to its caller. Read D1’s phrase “does not resurrect the emitter 030f deletes” as superseded by this correction. No production code or acceptance outcome changes.","timestamp":1786432274933102001}],"created_at":1786047439840444001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[],"description":"## Completion state\n\nThis story is complete. It removed duplicate workflow-owned CAS delivery loops by moving the behavior behind Python core entrypoints, while preserving best-effort defaults for existing callers. The implementation is merged in the three child tickets and is present at epic ref `694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d`.\n\n## Delivered architecture\n\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py` is the single owner of tickets-branch delivery mechanics:\n\n- `push_tickets_branch(..., strict=False)` owns push-first CAS retry, fetch-and-merge recovery, the five-cycle budget, and the one conditional terminal push.\n- Opt-in strict delivery raises `PushDeliveryError` with a stable reason, operational detail, and unpushed-commit count.\n- `commit_and_push_tickets_branch(...)` owns the locked stage, commit, and push transaction used by automation.\n- The private `python -m rebar._store.push` adapter translates typed core failures into process stderr and nonzero status; core functions remain reusable and never call `sys.exit`.\n- Workflow adapters select synchronous strict delivery explicitly. Existing library, CLI, MCP, importer, and ticket-store callers retain best-effort defaults.\n\nThe workflows now contain orchestration only:\n\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` invokes `rebar bridge run`; the Python bridge runner performs one locked strict commit-and-push action with the established bot identity and commit subject.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` invokes the Python push adapter once in strict mode.\n- Neither workflow contains its own fetch/merge/retry loop.\n- Strict workflow failure remains visible to GitHub Actions through a nonzero adapter result after the shared core exhausts delivery.\n\nThis is a Python strangler migration, consistent with the projects architecture. YAML remains the workflow declaration layer, while locking, CAS recovery, retry policy, staging, committing, error classification, and diagnostics live in Python. No new shell script or shell-only runtime path was introduced.\n\n## Child ownership and merged evidence\n\n- `953a-d616-52d3-4efe` owns strict push semantics, the corrected CAS retry budget, typed failure reasons, and the initial push-only Python adapter. Merged commit: `a8e0f67c827706525becb9b4d3df8a4da738d44e`.\n- `2bc9-75aa-a08a-478a` owns the locked `commit-and-push` entrypoint, dirty-tree handling, commit identity/message, and reuse of the strict push contract. Merged commit: `1532700efd`.\n- `742d-54f1-3b6f-4864` owns both workflow cutovers and removal of the stale raw-git/CAS-loop rationale. Merged commits: `11a026540b`, `039fe7bbde`, and `511c339a63`.\n\nAll three children are closed and completion-certified by principal `b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126` at ref `694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d`.\n\n## Interface, installation, and legacy continuity\n\nThe change deliberately preserves the established interfaces:\n\n- Python library: the strict flag is keyword-only and defaults false; existing public imports and write calls keep their return/disposition contract.\n- CLI: public commands continue through the same store/library seams; internal automation uses packaged Python entrypoints for strict delivery.\n- MCP server: MCP write tools continue through the same Python facade/store path with best-effort defaults, so protocol behavior does not diverge from the library.\n- Automated callers: bridge workflows opt into strict delivery; import, claim, transition, delete, compact, and event append remain best effort unless explicitly adapted.\n- Dual direction: read behavior and reconciliation input are unchanged; the migration affects outbound tickets-branch delivery only.\n\nInstallation behavior also remains continuous:\n\n- brew and pip installations receive the same packaged Python CLI and core modules.\n- MCP-server installation receives the same library implementation used by CLI and direct Python callers.\n- No workflow depends on a repository-only shell helper, editable-install path, or ambient interpreter behavior.\n- Existing workflows and environments that use legacy command vocabulary, status/exit vocabulary, best-effort delivery, bot identity, or commit subjects continue to work. Strictness is explicit at the two workflow boundaries, not silently imposed globally.\n\nNo persisted ticket schema or event vocabulary changed.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nThe child changes passed Gerrit LLM review and CI before merge. The combined oracle covers:\n\n- successful delivery after deterministic concurrent non-fast-forward races in real temporary git repositories;\n- rejected delivery after the retry budget, with typed core failure and nonzero strict adapter behavior;\n- policy-decline versus transport-failure classification;\n- lock timeout, store-epoch guards, narrow refspec, working-tree preservation, and stash restoration;\n- successful strict commit-and-push with the requested author and subject;\n- failed push leaving the local commit recoverable and reporting the unpushed count;\n- default best-effort behavior for legacy Python/library and MCP/store callers;\n- both workflow entrypoints delegating exactly once with no workflow-local retry loop;\n- focused workflow/core tests plus repository lint, typecheck, and full test gates.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] Python core is the sole owner of CAS push/retry behavior.\n- [x] The locked Python commit-and-push operation stages, commits, and delivers the workflow transaction.\n- [x] The bridge workflow delegates delivery once through the strict Python runner.\n- [x] The canary workflow delegates delivery once through the strict Python push adapter.\n- [x] No workflow-local fetch/merge/retry loop remains.\n- [x] Exhausted strict delivery produces a typed core failure and a nonzero automation result.\n- [x] Existing Python library callers remain best effort by default.\n- [x] Existing CLI callers remain compatible.\n- [x] Existing MCP callers remain compatible.\n- [x] brew, pip, and MCP-server installations all execute the same packaged Python implementation.\n- [x] Legacy vocabulary, environments, bot identity, and commit-subject contracts remain usable.\n- [x] Success, contention, and rejected-push paths are covered by observable tests.\n- [x] Gerrit review and CI are green for every merged child change.\n\n## Rollback\n\nRollback is coordinated: restore the previous workflow delivery blocks before reverting the strict Python adapter/core entrypoints. No data migration is required because no persisted schema changed. The local commits produced before a strict push failure remain recoverable and can be delivered after the remote or policy issue is corrected.","env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_store/push.py","reason":"strict five-retry CAS semantics and locked commit-back entrypoint"},{"path":".github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml","reason":"replace inline CAS loop with strict core entrypoint while retaining behavior"},{"path":".github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml","reason":"replace client-copied inline CAS loop with best-effort core entrypoint"},{"path":"scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py","reason":"verify workflow migration removes raw git writes without exemptions"},{"path":"scripts/canary_bridge.py","reason":"update standing decision after core extraction"},{"path":"tests/scripts/test_reconcile_bridge_push_retry.py","reason":"re-home contention contract and reverse obsolete extraction decision"},{"path":"tests/unit/test_push_strict.py","reason":"strict versus best-effort failure matrix"},{"path":"tests/interfaces/store/test_push_contention.py","reason":"real-git five-competitor convergence oracle"},{"path":"docs/concurrency.md","reason":"document strict opt-in and best-effort compatibility"},{"path":"docs/jira-sync-setup.md","reason":"document migration and rollback for existing copied workflows"},{"path":"CHANGELOG.md","reason":"announce client template shape and continuity path"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_reopened_at":1786444698361228001,"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["parent","2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","parent_status_uuid":"62f9516b-521c-4358-bee4-0e242e51184e","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":["bridge"],"ticket_id":"1c1b-1da2-7ff4-4770","ticket_type":"story","title":"Collapse the duplicated CAS push loop into core","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["7e342ff7-2c0f-48b8-b15c-e6c55f00bcf6","2689ecb0-1953-4557-a12a-f216380f6fc7","599aad3d-0469-4aed-979e-9f434b720c41","ed75e827-1f18-4498-a383-88f8f9ae3a7e","9fbc7813-771b-44e9-8e8b-7b1cbfdd2f3a","ca9ac830-29d9-466a-b911-27f5fd2d0d22","2a55d7da-a546-4887-b6bc-cfabd2a2645c","d677fd1f-8912-4a3f-97a8-4689cdb0439a","4f41dc51-8cac-4eda-8399-cda814a604a3","53b8b4fe-4cd4-4a08-996a-358891a9047d","8e4a033e-701c-4316-a5ee-bd18e17a39a1","c5a5de80-c344-4a58-9f61-816fdd7c6edb","62f9516b-521c-4358-bee4-0e242e51184e","e7c1145e-9e0b-48bc-8da8-cd20ac271a75"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1786659531679376001,"uuid":"5062f440-5d65-4b2b-afa4-f5070ba453b9"}